---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import "../../../styles/story.css";
import ReadingModes from "../../../components/ReadingModes.astro";
import Cite from "../../../components/Cite.astro";
import QuoteCard from "../../../components/story/QuoteCard.astro";
import StorySources from "../../../components/StorySources.astro";
import { event, n, closes, marks } from "../../../data/midterms-2026-2026-11-07-trump-election-3-trillion-market-loss.mjs";
const votersStory = "/events/midterms-2026/2026-11-03-why-voters-turned-on-republicans/";

const W = 460, H = 270, L = 58, R = 12, T = 24, B = 38;
const lo = 7300, hi = 7720;
const x = (i) => L + (i * (W - L - R)) / (closes.length - 1);
const y = (v) => T + ((hi - v) * (H - T - B)) / (hi - lo);
const line = closes.map((p, i) => `${i ? "L" : "M"}${x(i).toFixed(1)},${y(p.value).toFixed(1)}`).join(" ");
const ticks = [7300, 7400, 7500, 7600, 7700];
const fmt = (v) => v.toLocaleString("en-US", { minimumFractionDigits: 2, maximumFractionDigits: 2 });
---
<BaseLayout title={`${event.title} | The Catch`} current="Events">
  <div slot="locator" class="locator"><a href="/events/">Series</a><a href="/events/midterms-2026/">2026 midterms</a><span>November 7, 2026</span></div>
  <div class="content">
    <main class="story" id="story-root">
      <p class="story-kicker" data-layer="fact">2026 midterms &middot; November 7, 2026 &middot; updated {event.updated}</p>
      <h1 data-layer="fact">{event.title}</h1>
      <p class="story-dek" data-layer="fact">{event.dek}<Cite c="trump-dumocrats" /><Cite c="chips-led-thursday" /><Cite c="hinojosa-moratorium-campaign" /><Cite c="dem-winners-restrict" /></p>
      <ReadingModes />
      <nav class="story-toc" aria-label="In this story" data-layer="fact">
        <details class="toc-disclosure" open><summary><span>In this story</span><span class="toc-current" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#what-happened">What happened</a></li>
          <li><a href="#how-big">How big the loss was</a></li>
          <li><a href="#what-moved">What moved stocks</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-catch">The Catch</a></li>
          <li><a href="#next">What happens next</a></li>
          <li><a href="#records">The records</a></li>
        </ol>
        </details>
      </nav>

      <section id="what-happened">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="1">On Thursday, Nov. 5, two days after <a href={votersStory}>Democrats won control of Congress</a>, U.S. stocks had their worst day since April 2025.<Cite c="dems-won-congress" /><Cite c="worst-since-april-2025" /> The S&amp;P 500 fell {n.thursdayPct}% and the Dow Jones Industrial Average {n.dowPoints} points, and the companies in the S&amp;P 500 lost an estimated ${n.sp500Loss} trillion in value in one day.<Cite c="sp-thursday-fall" /><Cite c="dow-nasdaq-thursday" /><Cite c="sp500-companies-2-9t" /> Chipmakers and other suppliers of data centers, the buildings full of computers that run artificial intelligence, led the fall. Several of the election's winners, including Texas's new Democratic governor-elect, had campaigned to pause or restrict new data centers.<Cite c="chips-led-thursday" /><Cite c="hinojosa-moratorium-campaign" /><Cite c="dem-winners-restrict" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="2">President Trump blamed the election. "The Stock Market crashed today because the Dumocrats had a big Tuesday," he posted that evening, and on Nov. 8 he wrote that the market "took a one day, 3 Trillion Dollar loss."<Cite c="trump-dumocrats" /><Cite c="trump-nov8-post" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="3">Most of the loss came back within days. Stocks rallied on Monday, Nov. 9, helped by a fall in oil prices, and on Friday, Nov. 13, the S&amp;P 500 closed at {n.nov13Close}, within {n.nov13Gap}% of where it stood on Election Day.<Cite c="monday-recovered" /><Cite c="sp-nov13" /><Cite c="sp-election-day-close" /></p>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>The S&amp;P 500 at each close, from Election Day to Nov. 13</strong></figcaption>
          <svg class="close-chart" viewBox={`0 0 ${W} ${H}`} role="img" aria-label={`S&P 500 closing levels: ${closes.map((p) => `${p.label} ${fmt(p.value)}`).join(", ")}`}>
            {ticks.map((t) => (
              <g>
                <line x1={L} x2={W - R} y1={y(t)} y2={y(t)} class="grid" />
                <text x={L - 6} y={y(t) + 3} text-anchor="end" class="axis">{t.toLocaleString("en-US")}</text>
              </g>
            ))}
            {marks.map((m, i) => (
              <g>
                <line x1={x(m.at)} x2={x(m.at)} y1={T - 6} y2={H - B} class="mark" />
                <circle cx={x(m.at)} cy={T - 10} r="10" class="mark-dot" />
                <text x={x(m.at)} y={T - 5} text-anchor="middle" class="mark-num">{i + 1}</text>
              </g>
            ))}
            <path d={line} class="series" />
            {closes.map((p, i) => (
              <g>
                <circle cx={x(i)} cy={y(p.value)} r="4.5" class={p.date === "2026-11-05" ? "pt pt-low" : "pt"} />
                <text x={x(i)} y={H - B + 22} text-anchor="middle" class="axis">{i === 0 ? "Nov. 3" : p.date.slice(8).replace(/^0/, "")}</text>
              </g>
            ))}
          </svg>
          <ol class="mark-key">
            {marks.map((m) => <li>{m.text}<Cite c={m.c} /></li>)}
          </ol>
          <p class="chart-source">Closing levels from the Associated Press's daily index tables; the Nov. 11 close is Nov. 12's close plus that day's 26.98-point fall.{closes.map((p) => <Cite c={p.c} />)}</p>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section id="how-big">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">How big the loss was</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Trump's figure holds. Companies in the S&amp;P 500 lost ${n.sp500Loss} trillion by Dow Jones Market Data's estimate, and those in the broader S&amp;P 1500 lost ${n.sp1500Loss} trillion, the Financial Times reported.<Cite c="sp500-companies-2-9t" /><Cite c="sp1500-3-2t" /> The Wilshire 5000, an index of the whole U.S. stock market, fell {n.wilshirePct}% that day.<Cite c="wilshire-fall" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">In dollars the fall was a one-day record. As a share of the market's value it was the worst day since April 2025, but not among the S&amp;P 500's 20 worst days since 1923, by The Cross Check's count.<Cite c="sp500-companies-2-9t" /><Cite c="worst-since-april-2025" /><Cite c="outside-20-worst" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="what-moved">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What moved stocks</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The first reaction to the vote was a rally. On Wednesday, Nov. 4, the S&amp;P 500 rose {n.wedPct}%, its best day in three months, and the Dow 1,569 points. With Trump in the White House and Democrats about to run Congress, Washington would be divided, and investors traditionally like divided government.<Cite c="wednesday-best-day" /><Cite c="investors-like-divided" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Under that rally, chip and data-center stocks were already falling. Nvidia fell {n.nvidiaWed}% and Micron {n.micronWed}%, while Amazon and Microsoft, among the big companies that pay for the data-center build-out, rose about {n.cloudWed}%.<Cite c="wednesday-split" /> In Texas, Gina Hinojosa, the Democrat who beat Gov. Greg Abbott, campaigned on an immediate moratorium on data centers, and Wells Fargo estimates that Texas holds {n.texasShare}% of the data-center capacity planned in the U.S.<Cite c="hinojosa-moratorium-campaign" /><Cite c="texas-27-percent" /> Democrats also won the governorships of Ohio and Georgia, where Amy Acton and Keisha Lance Bottoms had campaigned on restricting data centers.<Cite c="dem-winners-restrict" /> In local ballot measures the same day, voters favored limiting data centers in {n.ballotFor} of {n.ballotAll}, as Ballotpedia counted them on Nov. 6.<Cite c="ballot-40-of-41" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">It took about a day for the market's reaction to turn, Bloomberg reported.<Cite c="bloomberg-24-hours" /> Qualcomm, a chipmaker, had issued an earnings forecast after Wednesday's close that CNBC called light, with its sales of chips for phones down 26%, and its stock fell about {n.qualcommThu}%, leading chip stocks lower.<Cite c="qualcomm-forecast" /> On Thursday, Hinojosa told CNN, "we need to stop what we are doing with data centers until we have those protections."<Cite c="hinojosa-thursday" /> Chip stocks led the fall, with the Philadelphia Semiconductor Index, a benchmark for the industry, down more than {n.chipIndexThu}%, while Alphabet and Microsoft fell less than the market. Every one of the S&amp;P 500's 11 sectors ended lower.<Cite c="chips-led-thursday" /><Cite c="thursday-broad" /> Leveraged exchange-traded funds, which multiply investors' bets on a group of stocks, added to the selling: JPMorgan estimates they sold more than ${n.etfSold} billion of stock exposure that week, about {n.etfTech}% of it in tech.<Cite c="leveraged-etfs" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Joe Maher, an economist at the research firm Capital Economics, tied the fall to the election's winners in a report that day.<Cite c="maher-trigger" /></p>
        <QuoteCard speaker="Joe Maher, Capital Economics" when="Nov. 5, 2026" where="Research note, quoted by CBS News">
          <p>"The trigger for these moves appears to be the concern that the Democrats, both in Congress and at the state level, will seek to regulate the AI industry and restrict data center development."<Cite c="maher-trigger" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The fall also restarted an older slide. Chip stocks had been sliding for weeks: the chip index had dropped more than {n.chipSinceOct}% since the start of October, and Thursday's fall restarted a selloff that had paused just before the vote.<Cite c="chip-index-october" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="the-catch">
        <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
          <p class="tt-label">The Catch</p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <h3>The election moved stocks, through one industry</h3>
              <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="4">Trump has the size of the loss and its link to the election largely right.<Cite c="sp500-companies-2-9t" /><Cite c="maher-trigger" /> He says investors "throughout the World hated seeing what took place on Election Day" and did not want "to invest with these people."<Cite c="trump-nov14-post" /><Cite c="trump-douthat" /> The reasons named that day, and the stocks that fell most, point to something narrower: concern that the winners would pause or slow new AI data centers.<Cite c="chips-led-thursday" /><Cite c="maher-trigger" /><Cite c="ap-moratorium-worries" /><Cite c="bloomberg-24-hours" /> The idea of a pause was not only a Democratic one. Abbott, a Republican who is governor until January, had already frozen environmental permits and grid connections for data centers in Texas during an audit, and Ohio's Republican nominee for governor, Vivek Ramaswamy, promised a pause on new data centers on his first day.<Cite c="abbott-paused" /><Cite c="hinojosa-takes-office" /><Cite c="ramaswamy-pause" /></p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="next">
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happens next</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="5">Trump acknowledged the recovery on Nov. 14, posting that "the Market has since gone up."<Cite c="trump-since-gone-up" /> Hinojosa takes office Jan. 19 and has promised a moratorium on data-center approvals until the Texas Legislature passes laws to protect Texans.<Cite c="hinojosa-takes-office" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="records">
      <StorySources event={event.slug} />
      </section>
    </main>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .close-chart { width: 100%; max-width: 600px; height: auto; display: block; margin: 0.4rem 0 0.2rem; overflow: visible; }
  .close-chart .grid { stroke: var(--rule); stroke-width: 1; }
  .close-chart .axis { font: 500 15px var(--sans); fill: var(--slate); }
  .close-chart .series { fill: none; stroke: var(--blue); stroke-width: 2.6; }
  .close-chart .pt { fill: var(--blue); }
  .close-chart .pt-low { fill: var(--red, #b3261e); }
  .close-chart .mark { stroke: var(--gold, #9a6200); stroke-width: 1; stroke-dasharray: 3 3; }
  .close-chart .mark-dot { fill: var(--gold, #9a6200); }
  .close-chart .mark-num { font: 700 13px var(--sans); fill: #fff; }
  .mark-key { margin: 0.4rem 0 0; padding-left: 1.4rem; font: 500 0.85rem/1.45 var(--sans); color: var(--ink); }
</style>
